  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Empowered Miskito Women Conserving the Central American Scarlet Macaw</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project is intended to conserve the two most endangered parrots in Honduras, the Central American scarlet macaw and the great green macaw. About 500 scarlet macaws (ara macao cyanoptera), recently listed as Endangered on the U.S. Endangered Species Act with an estimated 1,500 individuals, depend upon the Moskitias forests. Specific activities include: (1) training 20 Miskito women from six local villages in forest patrols, handi-crafts and as environmental educators for parrot conservation; (2) supporting an indigenous womens ranger team to monitor and protect wild nest sites of macaws closer to the villages while the men continue patrols in more remote locations; (3) led by female rangers, develop wildlife and parrot clubs with local school children as well as a community parrot festival; and (4) selection of four women with leadership potential to participate as Ambassadors for the community and parrot conservation in a national and international biodiversity conference.</narrative>
  </description>
